Title: Dry Creek
Credit: Written by
Author: Previz Stage
Draft date: 2026-10-24

EXT. FRONTIER TOWN MAIN STREET - DAY

A single dirt street between weathered timber storefronts: a saloon, a feed store, a bank with a cracked sign. Heat shimmers off the ground. A wagon drawn by two horses rolls slowly past the water trough, trailing dust.

SHERIFF ADA REYES (40s, lean, sun-lined face, long black hair in a braid, a dusty brown duster over a vest, a tin star, a revolver on her hip) stands in the middle of the street.

ELI MARSH (20s, lanky, blond, a torn white shirt and suspenders, no hat, no gun) walks out of the saloon toward her and stops ten paces away.

REYES
You've got until that wagon clears the street.

ELI
I didn't take the money, Sheriff.

REYES
Then why'd you run?

ELI
(squinting into the sun)
Because everyone in this town already decided I did.

INT. DRY CREEK SALOON - DUSK

A long bar of dark wood, a cracked mirror behind it, oil lamps lit against the orange light slanting through the batwing doors. Overturned chairs. Sawdust on the boards.

Reyes sits at a table with a glass she hasn't touched. Eli stands at the bar.

ELI
You could've shot me.

REYES
I could have.

ELI
Why didn't you?

REYES
(turning the glass)
The bank manager's horse was tied up out back at midnight. Yours was in the livery.
